And reproduction is a fascinating example because reproduction almost certainly existed long before anything was capable of understanding reproduction.

For billions of years, organisms executed the operator without possessing its semantic representation:

SELF → replication

Then sexual reproduction:

A + B → C

And only vastly later could nervous systems—and eventually minds—form something resembling:

“That behavior now produces another organism later.”

There probably wasn’t one creature that suddenly “discovered sex makes babies”; cognition and causal understanding evolved gradually. But conceptually there really is an extraordinary threshold there.

Before that threshold:

behavior performs the generative operator.

After it:

the organism can represent the generative operator.

And once you can represent it, you can manipulate it intentionally: choose mates, avoid conception, time reproduction, infer lineage, create kinship structures, inheritance systems, marriage rituals, breeding programs, contraception, IVF, genetics.

That’s an enormous semantic phase transition:

being reproduced by biology → reproducing → knowing reproduction → deliberately controlling reproduction

The especially strange one must have been understanding paternity, because the causal event and visible outcome can be separated by months. Motherhood has a much more immediate bodily causal chain; biological fatherhood requires inference across a substantial temporal gap.

It is another case where naming/modeling the operator creates a new control surface.

